In theory yes (I think) -- but you have to tell Python or R that you're
dealing with an Eigen matrix that has a .data() method. Whereas
everything that supports wrapping C++ already knows about
vector<double>.

If the data in question is already flat, please use vector<double> and
have the Stan library do the mapping between the vector<double>'s memory
and Eigen MatrixXd's memory. That's in keeping with the way things
currently work in any event (e.g., var_context uses vector<double>).

Also, to the extent that we want to make the interface welcoming for
other software (Julia, etc.) it seems like sticking to just C++11 is a
good idea.

-a

