Andrew,

I agree that we don’t want to propagate bad models, but if we
were really serious about that then we wouldn’t be showing many
BUGS models at all!  The problem is that people want to use
these translations to _learn_ the Stan modeling language and
so they need direct translations in order to transfer their knowledge
from BUGS into Stan.  The _only_ way we’ll be able to have
any utility from this perspective is with two stages — first the direct
translation and only then the “proper” translation with modeling and
code improvements.

On Apr 8, 2015, at 1:11 AM, Andrew Gelman <gel...@stat.columbia.edu> wrote:

>
>> On Apr 7, 2015, at 8:04 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>>
>>> On Apr 8, 2015, at 9:55 AM, Andrew Gelman <gel...@stat.columbia.edu> wrote:
>>>
>>> I can see that Sebastian and others might want to do this.  But I don’t think such non-recommended models should be included in the official set of Stan models.
>>
>> I must be misunderstanding some subtlety here.  What do you take
>> to be the "official set of Stan models"?
>>
>> I thought you just recently said you were OK having direct translations
>> of BUGS models, gamma(0.001,0.001), normal(0,316....), and all,
>> as long as we put big warning labels on them with directions on where
>> to find the recommended alternatives.
>
> Yes, I said that, but now that I think about it, I don’t like it!
>
>

