Thanks Bob for the detailed answer. I think quite many of the BUGS models could be translated just following this example. I know that most of these suggestions can be found in the manual, but they are scattered around.

>   for (m in 1:M) {
>     mean_x[m] <- mean(col(x,m));
>     sd_x[m] <- mean(col(x,m));
>   }
>   for (n in 1:N)
>     z[n] <- (x[n] - mean_x) ./ sd_x;
> }
>
> But maybe that ./ is too complicated to read?
> Or maybe people don't like z[n] referring to a row
> of a matrix?

I think I'll wait for the slicing operator and then write

for (m in 1:M)

 z[:,m] <- (x[:,m] - mean(x[:,m])) / sd(x[:,m]);

> A critical piece of best practices is
> to define expressions only once and reuse them --- it's very
> important for efficiency, but can make things harder to read.

I think it's better to provide easy to read examples, and optimise for speed only when necessary. Naturally you could show some examples optimised for speed, but I don't think any of the BUGS examples are so slow that they would be good examples for that.

Aki

